<h2>The volume argument: Why you cannot just ride more</h2>

<p>The most persistent counter-argument to structured weight loss is that you do not need a plan or a calculated deficit. You just need to ride 15 hours a week, and the weight will fall off regardless of what you eat.</p>

<p>Mathematically, this is true. If you ride 15 hours a week, your energy expenditure is so massive that it is difficult to eat enough to maintain weight. But practically, this approach almost always fails.</p>

<p>First, very few amateur athletes have 15 hours a week to train. Second, drastically increasing your volume without managing your fuel intake inevitably leads to overtraining syndrome. Your endocrine system crashes, your testosterone drops, your cortisol stays elevated, and your sleep falls apart. Finally, massive unstructured volume triggers massive unstructured hunger. You end up eating back every calorie you burned, often in the form of highly processed foods.</p>

<p>Sustainable weight loss on the bike is not about maximizing the calories you burn. It is about controlling the deficit so you can still adapt to the training. Keep the deficit moderate, keep the endurance rides easy, lift weights to protect your muscle, and fuel the work you do on the saddle.</p>
